 * `DPLD_REQUIRE_SCAN` and `GITLEAKS_BIN` are read only by `tests/test_hygiene.py`. `DPLD_REQUIRE_SCAN` acts on any non-empty value (even `0`): it makes a missing gitleaks program a failure and also scans the git history. `GITLEAKS_BIN` is the path of the gitleaks program (if it is not set, the program is looked up on `PATH`). A hand-run secret scan must start in the repository root (`gitleaks dir .`, as `.github/workflows/lint.yml` does): the allowance for the lock files (`requirements-*.txt`) and the workflow files in `.gitleaks.toml` is anchored at the root, so a scan given an absolute folder path reports them.
 * `LEAD_DESK_PATH_ROOTS` (read by the load tools, `scripts/n8n_api.py import` and `tools/scrub_n8n_export.py`; not by the desk): extra folders, separated by `:` (`;` on Windows), where those tools may read and write the files named on their command line. Without it only the folder the tool was started in, the repository folder and the system temporary folder are allowed (`load_tools/README.md`). Not affected: `scripts/run_real_check.py`, which reads the config you give it from any folder (a file in a folder you can list), and the files named inside that config (`docs/real-services.md`, "Where the files may be").
 * `DPLD_REQUIRE_DEMO` is read only by `tests/conftest.py`. With `DPLD_REQUIRE_DEMO=1` a checkout that has no `demo_only` folder stops the test run at the start (exit code 2) instead of skipping the test files that need it (CI sets it, so a lost folder cannot make the run look green). Without it a missing folder skips each such test file as one item and the other files still run.
+
+### Exit codes
+
+One table, in one place: `EXIT_CODE_TABLE` in `src/lead_desk/constants.py`, one row per program, code and meaning. The programs: `server` (`python -m lead_desk.server`), `crash_desk`, `feed`, `reset_check`, `run_real_check` (the Python scripts), `reset.sh`, `reset.ps1`, `start_all.sh`, `start_all.ps1`. Inside one program a code has one meaning. A number can mean different things in different programs: 1, 2, 3, 4 and 5 (the pinned list `SAME_NUMBER_DIFFERENT_MEANING`), so read a number together with the program that returned it.
+
+* A refused `DESK_*` setting: 4 from the desk itself; 8 from every script that reads the settings itself (one plain line on the error stream, never the value). `crash_desk` gives 8 when its own reading refuses and the desk's 4 when the desk it runs refuses a late setting (a leadflow file, an approvers file, a sender address, a public bind address). `run_real_check` gives 8 for a refused setting, 2 for its own refusals (no config, a missing line, a database file that already exists), 1 when you do not answer yes, 3 when the check ran and failed.
+* `start_all.sh` and `start_all.ps1`: 1 the start did not complete (bad mode, pid files exist, `START_ALL_WAIT_SECONDS` not a number, the demo server did not answer, the desk was not ready in time, the desk ended with another code or with 0); 2 (only `start_all.sh`) it was run by a shell that is not bash; 3, 4 and 5 are the desk's own codes, passed on when the desk it started ended at once; 8 a setting was refused while the script read it.
+* `reset.sh` and `reset.ps1` (and `reset_check.py`, which they call): 2 refused before anything was stopped or deleted, 3 a process holds the database, 4 something still answers on a port, 5 a process did not stop in time, 6 a pid file names another program, 8 a setting was refused.
+* The desk: 1 the demo server (the sender) cannot be reached, or a Python traceback; 2 another desk holds the lock file; 3 the open-file limit is too low; 4 a setting was refused; 5 the port could not be bound. 137 is only the crash script, on purpose.
